@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import { parent model }
@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  task, task id, child run, child run state, domain event, result, launch spec,
    enqueue time, start time, child session, child driver,
}
@design/delegation-service.sigil from DelegationService import { queued task }
@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, project root, model endpoint,
}

component TaskStore {
  goal {
    Keep every task on disk so that it outlives the parent session and can be
    listed, attached and shown later.
  }

  state {
    The *store folder* is .agents/subzero/ in the project root. A *task record*
    is
    the set of files that TaskStore keeps in the directory tasks/<task id>/ of
    the
    store folder. It holds task.json with the frozen agent definition and, once
    the
    child run has started, the launch spec, the *event log* events.jsonl, and
    turns/1.json with the exact result the parent model received. A task whose
    child run
    failed before it started has no launch spec. The same directory also holds
    the
    *driver files* of the task, which are what its child driver keeps there
    beside
    the task record and which TaskStore neither writes nor removes.

    A task record also keeps the model endpoint and the enqueue time of its
    task
    from its first write, and the start time once its child run is released.

    The *task status* of a task is running while its child run state is
    Pending,
    Starting or Live. It then becomes the status of its result, which is
    completed,
    truncated, failed or cancelled, or it becomes abandoned when the
    *start-up sweep* marks it. A task whose task record holds a result has the
    task status of that result, whatever the latest line of the task index
    says.

    The *task index* is index.jsonl in the store folder. It is append-only and
    has
    one line for each update of a task status. The latest line for a task wins.
    A
    line records the task id, the agent, the task status, the
    *parent session id*
    when there is one, the child session, the file that holds it and the
    timestamps. It also records the *owner process*, which is the process that
    created the task and runs its child run, by its process id and its
    start time,
    on the first line written for the task. A parent session id is the id of
    the
    conversation that made the delegation, and a delegation from the command
    line
    has none.

    The child session of each task is kept in the *sessions folder* next to the
    task records.
  }

  logic {
    Run the start-up sweep at start-up and again whenever a caller asks for it:
    scan the task index for a task whose latest line says running and whose
    owner process is no longer alive, or is alive with a different start time.
    Mark that task abandoned by appending the change to the task index. The
    sweep signals no process, and it ends when every such task is marked. An
    abandoned task has no result and can still be listed and attached. Read the
    latest line of a task again just before appending, and append nothing for a
    task whose task record holds a result.

    When a task record is written with a new task status, write the result into
    the task record first, in one step, and only then append a line for it to
    the task index, which ends the write.
  }

  constraints {
    A task record must outlive the parent session that created it.

    The task index must be read as the latest line per task, and a corrupt
    trailing
    line must be skipped with a warning. Each line must be appended as a single
    write, so that two processes never interleave one line, and an abandoned
    line
    written twice must change nothing.

    The result in a task record must be written in one step, so that a reader
    never sees part of it, and before the line of the task index that gives its
    task status. A later abandoned line for a task whose task record holds a
    result must be ignored by every reader and never rewritten.

    TaskStore must never store a queued task as a task status of its own. A
    task whose task record has
    no start time and no result keeps the task status running until the
    start-up sweep marks it abandoned, so that the sweep and every listing
    read it as they read any running task.

    The start time must be kept where a reader in another process sees it
    through the task index or the task record, from the moment of the release.

    TaskStore must receive the project root from its caller and must not
    resolve
    one of its own, so that the store folder is the one the start-up sweep
    read.

    The event log must hold each domain event of the task across its child run,
    and
    it must never be sent to the parent model.

    The store folder must be created readable by its owner only, with a
    .gitignore
    inside it that ignores everything in it, so that each task record, the
    sessions folder and the task index never reach a commit,
    because the event log and the sessions can hold secrets from tool output.
    An
    existing .gitignore there must be left unchanged, and the user's own
    .gitignore
    must never be edited.
  }

  cases {
    The task index keeps the latest line for a task after three status updates,
    and
    a corrupt last line is skipped with a warning.

    A task whose latest line says running and whose owner process is dead at
    start-up is marked abandoned, and a running task whose owner process is
    alive is left running. A task whose owner process dies after the server
    started is marked abandoned by the next sweep that a caller asks for.

    A task whose owner process died after it wrote its result, but before it
    appended its settled line, is read as settled with the task status of that
    result, and a sweep that appended an abandoned line after the settled line
    leaves it settled.

    The start time written at the release of a child run is read back by a
    listing in a second process, and a task whose child run is Pending in a
    dead owner process is marked abandoned by the next start-up sweep, because
    its task status is running.

    The first write of a task record creates the store folder with its own
    .gitignore, and in a git repository nothing in it shows in git status while
    the
    user's own .gitignore is unchanged.
  }

  interface {
    Provide the task record of a task id for writing and reading, including its
    launch spec and its result. Create the store folder, readable by its owner
    only
    and ignoring itself, on the first write of a task record.

    Append each domain event to the event log of its task.

    Provide the model endpoint, the enqueue time and the start time of each
    task record to a reader in any process.

    List each task record of one parent session id, or of every
    parent session id,
    using the task index, each with its task status.

    TaskStore provides the sessions folder, where the child session of each
    task is
    kept, and the folder of the driver files of each task beside its
    task record,
    for TaskCore to hand to the child driver of that task.

    Run the start-up sweep for a caller at its start-up and whenever it asks
    again, and mark each task whose owner process is gone as abandoned.
  }

  decisions {
    The owner process is recorded because a run without an exit record is also
    what
    a healthy run looks like, so a sweep that did not check its owner would
    mark
    the live run of another process abandoned. The store folder ignores itself
    so
    that the secrets in it are protected without editing a file the user owns,
    and
    abandoned is its own task status because no parent model ever receives a
    result
    for
    such a task.
  }
}
